  "answer_to_user": "Straight answer first: I can't confirm a true 25-year (multi-decade) dividend-aristocrat streak from my data. My dividend-reliability signal only looks back over a roughly 10-year window and caps the no-cut streak at 10 years — so I have no way to verify that a company raised its dividend every year for 25+ years, and I won't quote a streak I can't see. What I CAN offer is the closest honest proxy: companies that have gone the full ~10-year tracked window with no dividend cut (streak = 10, clean through the 2020 and 2022 stress years). Examples: Western Union (WU.US) ~12.9% yield, PennantPark Floating Rate (PFLT.US), Community Healthcare Trust (CHCT.US, a REIT), Starwood Property Trust (STWD.US), and Hansae Yes24 (016450.KO) — all showing a 10-year no-cut streak. Note that a clean no-cut streak isn't the same as raising every year, and some of these (CHCT payout ~1.44, STWD ~1.25) pay out more than they earn on an EPS basis, which is normal for REITs/BDCs but worth checking on a cash-flow basis. For a verified 25-year aristocrat/king list you'd need a dedicated dividend-history source; from here, 'clean for 10y+ (the full tracked window)' is the most I can honestly stand behind. Not advice."
